earlier this week I interviewed David  Wallace wells about the climate and the  the sort of urgent nature of what is  taking place currently and the seemingly  intractable situation that were up  against with when it comes to protecting  the environment and protecting humanity  from humanity effectively and the effect  that we are having on the climate and on  the planet via industrialization and  pollution and burning of fossil fuels  for today's classic interview we're  going to go back to my interview with  David Keith from a couple of years ago  where we spoke about geoengineering we  didn't speak about geoengineering in the  conspiratorial sense we spoke about it  in the sense of as a climate change  mitigating mechanism let's check out  that interview right now I'm joined  today by David Keith who is professor of  engineering and of public policy at  Harvard University you've worked in the  fields of climate science energy  technology you've been in public policy  it's so interesting now what we are  seeing with regard to it I think that  geoengineering is sort of crossing a lot  of these areas that you've worked in and  it's touching on a lot of different  aspects that are increasingly present  sort of in the national narrative let's  start for people who don't know what  we're talking about what's  geoengineering so the idea of solar  geoengineering is the idea that humanity  might deliberately make the earth a  little bit more reflective by some  technological means like putting some  kind of aerosol a fine dust into the  stratosphere perhaps and that that would  partially and imperfectly and in  permanently offset some of the risks the  climate risks that come from the  accumulation of carbon dioxide of the  atmosphere and is this just in terms of  preventing temperature increases or are  there other potential benefits certainly  not just temperature climate changes a  whole host of things from changes in  extreme temperature changes in rainfall  patterns changes in sea level changes in  the impacts on the natural environment  on species and ecosystems and the  evidence which certainly comes with lots  of uncertainty but the evidence is that  a moderate amount of solar to measuring  something that say cut the overall rate  of climate change or warming if you like  in half would reduce changes both the  temperature but also reduce changes  increases in precipitation perhaps  increase reducing the increase in  extreme storms that are the things  pretty clearly destructive to the  poorest people in the world specifically  how would this be done in other words  what would be the sort of technological  mechanisms that would be used to carry  out such a policy so in some ways that's  the easiest part the hard questions are  mostly all about governance and science  well we'll get to that I think it's yeah  exactly but and it's reasonably clear  that at least in this lawn method  putting aerosols in the stratosphere  that really existing technology  essentially existing aircraft  technologies could be used to put say  you know some amount that will wrap up  from zero gradually to maybe over sort  of 25 or 50 years to something like a  million tons a year of material going  into the stratosphere and that that  could be done really with off-the-shelf  aircraft technology that many nations  could access and could be supplied by  many vendors so kind of variants of the  technology that you see in a business  jet nothing all that fancy so the basic  ability again my girl says try sphere is  something that can be done in Causton  aren't very different from the cost of  shipping materials by arif rate across  the Pacific one of the criticisms that  I've read of this type of geoengineering  is that although it might be effective  in terms of the mitigation of climate  change that there are all sorts of other  negative potential environmental  repercussions depending on what  specifically is used as an aerosol have  the developments accelerated in terms of  solving that issue over the last several  years versus maybe in the early 90s when  there were earlier discussions of this  technology  I think the big perspective here is that  because of concerns some I think very  well found illegitimate and some may be  a little bit kind of at the gut level  because of concerns about this  technology there isn't a large organized  research program so for example although  the United States is often seen as a  place of sort of technological optimism  justifiably so whether though there's a  huge overall budget for climate research  of several billion the year not counting  all the money that we spend it we should  spend much more in my opinion on cutting  emissions  there has been no organized US  government research on any kind consoler  dream of sharing and very little  globally there's small research programs  now and in Europe and China and some in  the US I helped to run one but they're  funded dominantly by philanthropic money  these stars is because there's been very  very little government money so that the  important answer at this point is that  we don't know very much there's there's  plenty and I can tell you someone we do  know but to be clear the argument that I  and I think many people like me are  making is not that we ought to do Gino  missionary I think that would be  ridiculous the argument is that we ought  to have a serious coordinated open  access research program to understand  more about how well would work what the  risks are out and controlling on the  internet if you were to for example  prepare with an intern for an interview  with someone like you and research  geoengineering you would inevitably come  across all sorts of conspiracy theories  about geoengineering sometimes the term  climate engineering is used you will  then probably go down the internet  rabbit hole into chemtrails and I've  interviewed one or two of the the  chemtrails conspiracy theories how did  this start  that geoengineering became so tied into  some of the I guess I would call them  not as not making a value judgment but  merely from the way that that the  material is presented some of the  juiciest conspiracy theories on the  Internet how did this develop I don't  know is the short answer I think from  what I understand there were long held  beliefs by probably a very tiny number  of people that the u.s. government was  deliberately manipulating the ionosphere  through something called the HARP  program which was actually a pretty  normal nerdy physics program to try and  beam microwaves into the ionosphere and  and a bunch of related programs that  people who kind of want to see some  nefarious coordinated action imagined  were happening and then as the more  public discussion of solar juma sharing  began to be more visible say a decade  ago those two ideas were somehow  conflated I don't know why what I think  is clear is that the extraordinary  spread of social media has really  changed the way these things work so  that there's much more you know it's  much easier to produce fake news as  we've seen in in lots of countries  including the US the last few years it's  much easier to produce materials that  appear too naive viewers to be  convincing and so you can get many more  people falling into the trap of  believing stuff that you know  objectively to the mainstream seems  really bizarre and and extraordinarily  unlikely to be true to it and that's  true on all sorts of cases but this is  certainly a particularly odd one and it  stirs up a pretty high level of hatred  and anger in the Internet and I think it  probably will affect the kind of normal  discussion of jima sharing which itself  is controversial but in totally  different ways let's talk now a little  bit about the sort of politics and  governance issues around this I mean  certainly I think we can all imagine how  if you go up into the sky and you start  spraying aerosols or whatever it is  ultimately used those aerosols are not  going to respect to these imaginary  lines on maps called country borders so  logistically who needs to agree to such  a program what are the sort of  governance obstacles that that would be  presented  that's a very hard question it's it's a  it's an emerging question I mean for all  of these global public goods and I think  that's ultimately what this is if it  makes any sense  including goods that of course like any  of them have some risks and side effects  the way we manage them is in emerging  isn't it was a work in progress so  that's true from managing pollution of  the ozone layer it's true for match in  global climate it's true for for  managing infectious diseases and all  sorts of things so there isn't a simple  formula for what's legitimate obviously  international organizations matter but  soda coalition's of individual countries  and I think it will be some mixture of  what some individual countries do what  environmental groups or other  transnational public organizations do  and and what the international relations  like the United Nations do so it's  really hard to guess what I think is  really hopeful is there are now serious  emerging efforts to look at what it how  that governance would work so for  example a guy called Janos pasture who  is the lead advisor to the  secretary-general for climate is now  running an effort to try and develop  governance for solar generation area one  of the concerns that I have that I know  is shared by at least some people in my  audience with regard to whatever we may  ultimately decide to do to try to deal  with human caused climate change is that  rather than taking a development like  geoengineering or a new form of energy  whatever the case may be and saying okay  now we can use this to actually make a  net improvement to what we're doing to  the climate that it will be seen at  least by some as sort of carte blanche  to keep doing exactly what we're doing  right saying hey now instead of just  improving the situation we'll keep doing  what we're doing but we won't be doing  as much damage because we've come up  with this new thing how do we deal with  that well I mean I think that is the the  central fear that it beyond any other  has held back researching this top  and and I think there's ways in which  it's both deeply legitimate and and some  ways that it's less so so yeah of course  there will be some interests interest  for example in parts of the fossil fuel  industry they would like to keep the  party going and not reduce the fossil  fuel consumption the hard truth is that  if you want to stable climate you have  to bring fossil fuel consumption to zero  eventually and nothing about solar do  you measure any changes that you must  bring emissions to zero in a net solar  generation can reduce the risks of  climate change for co2 it's already in  the air but it can't eliminate them and  so I think it's certainly true that some  forces allied with say the fossil fuel  industry that doesn't want to see  hundred regulation might use the  possibility of solar Jewish sharing as  an excuse for inaction but the question  is how powerful those forces really be  in the end the political forces that  want to see our climate protected need  to win anyway and I think gradually we  we are beginning to win we are beginning  to win the battle to restrict co2  emissions and I don't think that adding  something that further reduces perhaps  significantly reduces the risks to the  most vulnerable in the planet which is  what solar generation could do I don't  think that in the long game that  suddenly means that we lose the  political ability to cut emissions but I  agree it's a legitimate and hard  question all right something we will be  dealing with certainly for years to come  we've been speaking with David Keith  professor of engineering and of public  policy at Harvard University thanks so  much for talking to us about this thanks  a lot it's great to be on your show

